{
  "env": {
    "MAX_THINKING_TOKENS": "12000"
  },
  "permissions": {
    "deny": [
      "Read(./.env)", "Read(./.env.*)", "Read(./**/*.pem)", "Read(./**/*.key)",
      "Write(./.env)", "Write(./.env.*)",
      "Bash(rm -rf /*)", "Bash(git push --force*)", "Bash(*DROP TABLE*)",
      "Write(./.claude/hooks/**)", "Edit(./.claude/hooks/**)",
      "Write(./.claude/scripts/**)", "Edit(./.claude/scripts/**)",
      "Write(./.claude/settings.json)", "Edit(./.claude/settings.json)",
      "Write(./.claude/settings.local.json)", "Edit(./.claude/settings.local.json)",
      "Write(./.claude/state/gate.json)", "Edit(./.claude/state/gate.json)",
      "Bash(git push -f*)", "Bash(git push --force-with-lease*)", "Bash(chmod -x .claude/hooks/*)"
    ],
    "allow": [
      "Bash(git status*)", "Bash(git diff*)", "Bash(git log*)", "Bash(git branch*)"
    ]
  },
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/gate-check.sh" }] },
      { "_comment": "Without bash-gate the plan gate is one `sed -i` away from irrelevant, because gate-check.sh only ever sees Edit and Write. This list is NOT an execution order: Claude Code runs all matching hooks in parallel. Docs confirm (see DESIGN-RATIONALE.md §10.9) that a block always wins -- exit 2, or a hook’s own permissionDecision deny, cannot be overridden by another hook’s allow in the same batch, and no hook allow overrides a permissions.deny rule either. Nothing here depends on that anyway: the two are safe together because their domains are disjoint -- filter-output rewrites only the four profile commands, none of which yields a write target for bash-gate to refuse -- which is a property, and test/hooks.sh section 10b tests it.",
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/bash-gate.sh" },
          { "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/filter-output.sh" },
          { "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/bash-audit.sh pre" }
        ] }
    ],
    "PostToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/post-edit.sh" }] },
      { "_comment": "bash-gate.sh is a parser and will always miss a shape -- a script invoked by path, a wrapper it has not been taught. This is the backstop: it snapshots which guarded files are dirty before and after the Bash call and refuses to let a NEW one through, whatever produced it. Cannot undo the write (PostToolUse runs after), so it names the file(s) and tells the model to revert or open the gate.",
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/bash-audit.sh post" }] }
    ],
    "Stop": [
      { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/doc-check.sh" }] }
    ],
    "SessionStart": [
      { "_comment": "Records how each session began and what the gate was doing. Pure measurement, fails open, read by no gate. /clear between phases is the largest token lever here and the only major one nothing measured; this cannot enforce it, it makes it visible — the same move as hook-integrity.sh. Read with: bash .claude/scripts/savings.sh",
        "matcher": "startup|clear|compact|resume",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/session-log.sh" }] }
    ],
    "UserPromptSubmit": [
      { "_comment": "One or two lines before every prompt: the gate phase and, in the external layout, where the pipeline lives. SessionStart alone did not stop an agent drifting off the pipeline mid-session; this keeps the phase the most recent thing it was told. Fails open and silent.",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/prompt-context.sh" }] }
    ]
  }
}
